#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: fence-doc-block
---

# A `@doc` block is documentation, not program

`@doc` on the fence line marks a block as documentation. It is still parsed and
diagnosed like any other block — that is the point of preferring it over an untagged
fence, which lets a usage example rot into something that no longer compiles — but it
is never executed, never lowered, and its bindings are invisible to other blocks.

Spec: `specs/ssc-doc-blocks.md`. This case must stay a CROSS-LANE case: per-lane
greens are exactly what hid the INT-vs-native divergence that motivated the feature.

```scalascript
def shared(): Int = 1
println("program")
```

The block below is documentation. It must print nothing on every lane, and its `demo`
must not become a program definition.

```scalascript @doc
def demo(): Int = shared() + 41
println("DOC-BLOCK-MUST-NOT-RUN")
```

A `@doc` block does not hide the blocks around it.

```scalascript
println(shared() + 1)
```
